<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Legion Grid Jolt physics region module — SLICE 1 SKELETON.

    RC-native project (NOT a verbatim lift of Legion's csproj): discovery is DotNetCorePlugins +
    IPluginRegistryProvider (see PluginRegistration.cs), NOT Mono.Addins. Do not add a Mono.Addins
    reference or an [assembly: Addin] manifest — they are dead in this tree and fail discovery silently.

    Native joltc.dll is shipped the RC way — runtimes/<rid>/native/ + the <Content> glob below — exactly
    like ubODE (ubode.dll) and BulletSim (BulletSim.dll). Under a PORTABLE publish (no -r) this copies the
    native into the output automatically, the same reason SkiaSharp's native lands. This is an IMPROVEMENT
    over Legion, where the csproj pulled the STOCK NuGet joltc and deploy-jolt-legion.ps1 overwrote it with
    the patched build post-build; here the patched native is a first-class, checked-in build artifact.

    ★ The vendored joltc.dll is the PATCHED build (per-system TempAllocator, SHA-256 16AF7638…). The
    per-instance _simLock in the backend (slice 2) REQUIRES it — stock joltc + instance locks =
    "TempAllocator: Freeing in the wrong order" -> abort the moment two regions step. assert-patched-joltc.ps1
    guards the published copy so a stray NuGet restore can never reintroduce stock. Provenance +
    rebuild recipe: legion-grid-source/native/joltc/README.md.

    SLICE 2: the module now drives the real backend (ProjectReference to Legion.Physics below). It still
    takes NO direct JoltPhysicsSharp/JoltPhysics.Native PackageReference — the managed binding flows
    transitively through Legion.Physics, and the PATCHED native ships from the runtimes/ glob here while
    Legion.Physics excludes the STOCK native. So this project still adds zero NuGet-native surface of its
    own (no NU1605 RID-restore trap); it just owns the one real joltc.dll.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <AssemblyName>OpenSim.Region.PhysicsModules.LegionJolt</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>OpenSim.Region.PhysicsModules.LegionJolt</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyTitle>OpenSim.Region.PhysicsModules.LegionJolt</AssemblyTitle>
    <Company>Legion Grid</Company>
    <Product>OpenSim</Product>
    <Description>Legion Grid Jolt physics region module</Description>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>True</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../Source/OpenSim.Framework/OpenSim.Framework.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../Source/OpenSim.Region.Framework/OpenSim.Region.Framework.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../Source/OpenSim.Region.PhysicsModules.SharedBase/OpenSim.Region.PhysicsModules.SharedBase.csproj" />
    <!-- SLICE 2: the engine-agnostic Jolt backend (per-instance _simLock; requires the patched joltc
         shipped via runtimes/win-x64/native above). -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../Addons/LegionPhysics/Legion.Physics/Legion.Physics.csproj" />
    <!-- SLICE 3: the backend-agnostic Halcyon vehicle controller. Independent of Legion.Physics (no
         cycle); slice 4's JoltVehicleBody will implement its IVehicleBody onto the backend. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../Addons/LegionPhysics/Legion.Vehicles/Legion.Vehicles.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Ship the native the RC way: runtimes/<rid>/native/joltc.dll, copied to output by this glob.
         Portable publish carries all present RIDs; today only win-x64 exists (Legion never built a
         linux .so). Adding runtimes/linux-x64/native/libjoltc.so later is a drop-in for a Linux grid. -->
    <Content Include="runtimes\**\*.*">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="log4net" Version="3.3.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
